## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical firewall configuration skills on RHEL 9, requiring precise rule creation, persistence, and verification—essential for enterprise security administration.

## Key Concepts
- nftables ruleset syntax and packet flow
- Rule persistence via /etc/nftables.conf
- Service management with systemctl
- Connectivity testing from allowed vs. disallowed sources
- Verification using nft list ruleset

## Code Example
```bash
#!/usr/sbin/nft -f
flush ruleset
table inet filter {
  chain input {
    type filter hook input priority 0; policy drop;
    iifname "lo" accept
    ip saddr 192.168.1.0/24 tcp dport 22 accept
  }
  chain forward {
    type filter hook forward priority 0; policy drop;
  }
  chain output {
    type filter hook output priority 0; policy accept;
  }
}
```

## Implementation Steps
```bash
# Install nftables
dnf install -y nftables

# Create ruleset
cat > /etc/nftables.conf << 'EOF'
#!/usr/sbin/nft -f
flush ruleset
table inet filter {
  chain input {
    type filter hook input priority 0; policy drop;
    iifname "lo" accept
    ip saddr 192.168.1.0/24 tcp dport 22 accept
  }
  chain forward {
    type filter hook forward priority 0; policy drop;
  }
  chain output {
    type filter hook output priority 0; policy accept;
  }
}
EOF

# Enable and start services
systemctl enable nftables
systemctl start nftables
systemctl status nftables

# Verify rules
nft list ruleset

# Test connectivity (from allowed host)
ssh user@server-ip

# Test connectivity (from disallowed host)
ssh user@server-ip  # Should timeout/refuse
```

## Why This Is Asked
This scenario tests practical container containment, SELinux policy implementation, audit trail configuration, and host isolation in a realistic data ingestion workflow.

## Key Concepts
- Podman user namespaces for UID/GID isolation
- SELinux targeted policy modules for domain confinement
- Auditd path monitoring for comprehensive file I/O tracking
- Secure bind mounts with minimal host filesystem exposure

## Code Example
```bash
# Create SELinux module
module ingest_t 1.0;

require {
    type unconfined_t;
    type var_run_t;
    type etc_t;
    type default_t;
    class file { read write open getattr };
    class dir { search read open getattr };
    class sock_file { write open };
}

# Policy rules for ingest_t domain
allow ingest_t etc_t:file { read open getattr };
allow ingest_t var_run_t:sock_file { write open };
allow ingest_t default_t:dir { search read open getattr };
allow ingest_t default_t:file { read write open getattr };
```

## Implementation Commands
```bash
# Create dedicated user
sudo useradd -r -s /bin/false ingest
sudo usermod -v 100000-199999 -w 100000-199999 ingest

# Build and load SELinux module
cat > ingest.te << 'EOF'
module ingest_t 1.0;

require {
    type unconfined_t;
    type var_run_t;
    type etc_t;
    type default_t;
    class file { read write open getattr };
    class dir { search read open getattr };
    class sock_file { write open };
}

allow ingest_t etc_t:file { read open getattr };
allow ingest_t var_run_t:sock_file { write open };
allow ingest_t default_t:dir { search read open getattr };
allow ingest_t default_t:file { read write open getattr };
EOF

checkmodule -M -m -o ingest.mod ingest.te
semodule_package -o ingest.pp -m ingest.mod
sudo semodule -i ingest.pp

# Configure audit rules
sudo auditctl -w /opt/ingest/config -p r -k ingest_config
sudo auditctl -w /srv/ingest/data -p rwxa -k ingest_data
sudo auditctl -w /var/run/ingest-proxy.sock -p w -k ingest_ipc

# Run container
podman run \
  --uidmap 0:100000:65536 \
  --gidmap 0:100000:65536 \
  -v /opt/ingest/config:/opt/ingest/config:ro \
  -v /srv/ingest/data:/srv/ingest/data:rw \
  -v /var/run/ingest-proxy.sock:/var/run/ingest-proxy.sock \
  --network=none \
  --security-opt label=type:ingest_t \
  --user 1000 \
  ingest-service:latest
```
